---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/astro/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/astro/Footer.astro";
import ReportChart from "../../components/astro/ReportChart.astro";

// Original-research asset. Its primary job is to be CITED (journalists, newsletters,
// podcasts, AI answer engines), not to convert. Every design decision here follows from that:
// ungated, stat-first hero with no CTA, methodology visible rather than buried, data tables
// open rather than collapsed, exactly one soft CTA at the very end, inline SVG charts with no
// client JS so every number is real parseable text.
//
// SHIPPED noindex FOR FOUNDER REVIEW (2026-08-06). Flip to "index, follow" to publish. Doing
// so is the actual publish action; the distribution sequence in pitch-plan.md section 7 starts
// from there. Leaving it noindex would defeat the page's entire purpose, so do not copy the
// noindex default from the neighbouring funnel pages.
const ROBOTS = "noindex, follow";

const PAGE_URL = "https://swotbee.com/research/state-of-hubspot-renewals-2026/";
const STAT_PACK = "/reports/state-of-hubspot-renewals-2026-stat-pack.csv";
const PUBLISHED = "2026-08-06";

// One colour per pain category, held constant across all six charts so a reader learns the
// mapping once. Hex rather than Tailwind classes because these are SVG fill attributes.
const C = {
  renewal: "#C47A2A",   // v6-copper
  integration: "#5AAB9D", // v6-teal
  dataQuality: "#1A7AB5", // v6-blue
  commissions: "#D4960A", // v6-amber
  other: "#8A94A6",       // neutral: it is a catch-all, should not read as a real category
};

const findings = [
  { n: 1, text: "Renewal-process pain is the most frequently voiced problem in HubSpot renewals: <strong>129 of 392</strong> trusted customer-voice records (33%) describe it, ahead of integration, commissions, and data quality." },
  { n: 2, text: "Integration and accounting-sync pain is the most acute at any meaningful scale: <strong>64 of 92</strong> integration records (70%) are rated high-intensity. One smaller category rates higher (agency-overflow, 4 of 4 records), but on a base of four it is disclosed rather than ranked." },
  { n: 3, text: "Integration alone accounts for <strong>44% of all acute pain</strong>: 64 of the 147 high-intensity trusted records, more than renewal (42) and data quality (16) combined." },
  { n: 4, text: "Renewal and integration together make up <strong>56% of the corpus</strong>: 221 of 392 trusted records describe one or the other." },
  { n: 5, text: "HubSpot renewal integration pain is an accounting-sync problem: <strong>66 of 92</strong> integration records (72%) name NetSuite, Xero, or QuickBooks as the system that will not stay in sync." },
  { n: 6, text: "The accounting-sync theme is the most consistently severe slice of the study: <strong>38 of 43</strong> dedicated CRM-to-accounting records (88%) are rated high-intensity." },
  { n: 7, text: "One third of renewal complaints are already at crisis level: <strong>42 of 129</strong> renewal records (33%) are high-intensity, and the recurring trigger is HubSpot blocking auto-created renewal deals to \"prevent an infinite loop.\"" },
  { n: 8, text: "Commissions is a real but lower-acuteness pain: <strong>48 of 392</strong> records (12%) mention it, yet only 1 was rated high-intensity." },
];

const limitations = [
  ["Not a survey.", "No respondent count, no sampling frame, no margin of error. Frequencies describe our collected records, which over-represent forums where people go to complain."],
  ["Industry is unknown for most records.", "342 of 392 trusted records (87%) have no reliable industry tag, so we make no vertical-specific prevalence claims anywhere. SaaS is the best-represented known vertical (37 records) but that is a floor, not a measured share."],
  ["Role is unknown for 221 of 392 records.", "Where role is known (171 records), operations admins and RevOps leaders dominate (142 of 171, 83%), which shapes whose voice this study captures."],
  ["Snippet sources.", "Community records are sourced from search snippets (HubSpot Community blocks direct fetch), so quotes are verbatim from the snippet, not the full thread."],
  ["Reddit sentiment is a collector artifact.", "The reddit sentiment field is uniformly \"complaint\" by how it was gathered, so we do not analyze reddit sentiment anywhere."],
  ["LinkedIn attribution.", "Perplexity-LinkedIn records are anonymized to source-type and role; author affiliation was not independently re-verified, so we lean on them lightly."],
  ["The renewal bucket is not monolithic.", "Most renewal records are about teams trying to build their own renewal automation, but a minority (mostly on r/hubspot) are complaints about HubSpot's own contract auto-renewal and pricing. We flag this rather than blend the two."],
];

const toc = [
  { group: "Start here", items: [["#key-findings", "Key findings"], ["#methodology", "Methodology and limits"]] },
  { group: "The findings", items: [["#concentration", "Where pain concentrates"], ["#automation-gap", "The renewal-automation gap"], ["#line-items", "Line items and cloning"], ["#accounting-sync", "Why accounting-sync hurts most"], ["#visibility", "NRR and multi-year math"], ["#commissions", "Commissions"], ["#hiring", "What teams are hiring to fix"]] },
  { group: "Framework", items: [["#rli", "The Renewal Leakage Index"]] },
  { group: "Close", items: [["#what-it-means", "What this means"], ["#data", "Download the data"], ["#cite", "How to cite this report"]] },
];

const articleLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article",
  headline: "The State of HubSpot Renewals 2026",
  description: "A listening study of 545 real-world records on how renewals actually work in HubSpot.",
  datePublished: PUBLISHED, dateModified: PUBLISHED,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SwotBee", url: "https://swotbee.com" },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SwotBee", url: "https://swotbee.com" },
  mainEntityOfPage: PAGE_URL, isAccessibleForFree: true,
};

// Dataset schema is the single most direct lever for the "get cited by AI answer engines"
// goal, and none of the site's existing structured-data types covered it.
const datasetLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Dataset",
  name: "The State of HubSpot Renewals 2026 corpus",
  description: "545 hand-classified real-world records (392 trusted customer-voice) describing how renewal operations work in and around HubSpot, drawn from public practitioner discussion and first-party notes.",
  creator: { "@type": "Organization", name: "SwotBee", url: "https://swotbee.com" },
  datePublished: PUBLISHED, temporalCoverage: "../2026-07-05",
  license: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
  isAccessibleForFree: true, url: PAGE_URL,
  variableMeasured: [
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", name: "pain_category", description: "renewal, integration, commissions, data quality, agency overflow, other" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", name: "intensity", description: "low, medium, or high business impact" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", name: "source_type", description: "reddit, reviews, HubSpot Community, accounting-sync, internal, LinkedIn" },
  ],
  distribution: [{ "@type": "DataDownload", encodingFormat: "text/csv", contentUrl: `https://swotbee.com${STAT_PACK}` }],
};
---

<Layout
  title="The State of HubSpot Renewals 2026 | Original Research"
  description="A listening study of 545 real-world records on how renewals actually work in HubSpot. Renewal-process pain is the most common; accounting-sync is the most acute. Ungated data, full methodology."
  robots={ROBOTS}
>
  <Navbar />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleLd)} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(datasetLd)} />

  <article class="report max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">

    <!-- HERO: stat-first, no CTA, no logos. The restraint is itself the credibility signal. -->
    <header>
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-v6-copper">Original research &middot; August 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-2 mb-5 text-balance">
        The State of HubSpot Renewals 2026
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-v6-dark">129 of 392</strong> real HubSpot renewal complaints trace to the same
        mechanical cause: HubSpot can clone a deal, but not its line items, so renewal after renewal
        gets rebuilt by hand or quietly skipped.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
        We read 545 real-world records where teams describe how renewals actually work: Reddit threads,
        G2 and Capterra reviews, HubSpot Community posts, RevOps forums, job descriptions, and interview
        notes. This page is the full study, ungated, with the data behind every number.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- AT A GLANCE: answers "is this real" inside the first screen, before any scrolling. -->
    <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-px bg-v6-border border border-v6-border mt-8 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-white p-3.5"><dt class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-v6-text-muted">Records analysed</dt><dd class="text-xl font-bold text-v6-dark tabular-nums mt-0.5">545</dd></div>
      <div class="bg-white p-3.5"><dt class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-v6-text-muted">Trusted base</dt><dd class="text-xl font-bold text-v6-dark tabular-nums mt-0.5">392</dd></div>
      <div class="bg-white p-3.5"><dt class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-v6-text-muted">Collected</dt><dd class="text-xl font-bold text-v6-dark tabular-nums mt-0.5">2026-07-05</dd></div>
      <div class="bg-white p-3.5"><dt class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-v6-text-muted">Method</dt><dd class="text-sm font-bold text-v6-dark mt-1.5">Listening study,<br />not a survey</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <!-- Self-selecting wayfinding: two audiences, one page, no forked content. -->
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-v6-text-muted">
      <a href="#methodology" class="text-v6-copper font-semibold hover:underline">Verifying a stat? Methodology and data tables &rarr;</a>
      <span class="mx-2 text-v6-border">|</span>
      <a href="#key-findings" class="text-v6-copper font-semibold hover:underline">Reading for insight? Key findings &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <!-- A visible self-correction is a stronger trust signal than a claim of zero errors. -->
    <aside class="mt-6 border-l-4 border-v6-teal bg-v6-bg pl-5 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-lg">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-v6-dark mb-1.5">How we check our own numbers</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Before publishing, all eight findings were independently recomputed from the raw records. Seven
        reproduced exactly. One did not: an earlier draft called integration
        <em>"the highest acuteness of any pain category"</em>, but agency-overflow rates 4 of 4 (100%)
        against integration's 64 of 92 (70%). We corrected the claim and disclosed the smaller category
        rather than quietly dropping it. Finding 5 counts the tool-mention field specifically; counting
        quote text as well gives 68 of 92, so the figure we publish is the conservative one.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <nav class="mt-8 border border-v6-border rounded-xl bg-v6-bg p-5" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-v6-dark mb-3">On this page</p>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-4">
        {toc.map((g) => (
          <div>
            <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-v6-text-muted mb-1.5">{g.group}</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1 list-none p-0 m-0">
              {g.items.map(([href, label]) => (
                <li><a href={href} class="text-sm text-v6-dark hover:text-v6-copper hover:underline">{label}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-v6-border text-xs text-v6-text-muted">
        <a href={STAT_PACK} download class="text-v6-copper font-semibold hover:underline">&darr; Download the stat pack (CSV)</a>
        <span class="mx-2">&middot;</span>
        <button type="button" data-print class="text-v6-copper font-semibold hover:underline">Print or save as PDF</button>
      </p>
    </nav>

    <!-- KEY FINDINGS: every line restates its own base, so a shared deep link still makes sense. -->
    <h2 id="key-findings" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-2 scroll-mt-24">Key findings</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted mb-5">
      Self-contained and quotable. The base is stated on every line. Trusted customer-voice base is
      392 records unless noted otherwise.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-3">
      {findings.map((f) => (
        <li id={`finding-${f.n}`} class="finding relative border border-v6-border rounded-xl p-4 pr-12 bg-white scroll-mt-24">
          <span class="absolute left-4 top-4 text-xs font-bold text-v6-copper tabular-nums">{String(f.n).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed pl-7" set:html={f.text}></p>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="copy-stat absolute top-3 right-3 text-[11px] text-v6-text-muted border border-v6-border rounded px-2 py-1 hover:text-v6-copper hover:border-v6-copper transition-colors"
            data-anchor={`#finding-${f.n}`}
            aria-label={`Copy finding ${f.n} with its source`}
          >Copy</button>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <!-- METHODOLOGY: full and open. These disclosures are what make the numbers citable. -->
    <h2 id="methodology" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Methodology and honest limitations</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      545 active real-world records, collected and hand-classified by SwotBee from public practitioner
      discussion and first-party notes, accessed 2026-07-05. Each record carries a source, a pain
      category, a role tag, an industry tag, an intensity rating, and a short verbatim quote.
      <strong>This is a listening and content-analysis study, not a survey.</strong> We did not run a
      panel or ask anyone questions. Read every percentage as "share of records that describe X", not
      "share of people who answered X".
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-v6-border overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <caption class="text-left text-xs text-v6-text-muted p-3 pb-2">
          The three tiers. Prevalence claims are computed only on the trusted tier.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-v6-bg">
            <th scope="col" class="text-left font-semibold text-v6-dark px-3 py-2">Tier</th>
            <th scope="col" class="text-left font-semibold text-v6-dark px-3 py-2 tabular-nums">Records</th>
            <th scope="col" class="text-left font-semibold text-v6-dark px-3 py-2">Used for percentages?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border">Trusted customer-voice</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border tabular-nums font-semibold">392</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border text-v6-text-muted">Yes, every figure uses this base</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border">Job postings</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border tabular-nums">54</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border text-v6-text-muted">No, process evidence only</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border">Colour-only (vendor and consultant content)</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border tabular-nums">99</td><td class="px-3 py-2 border-t border-v6-border text-v6-text-muted">No, excluded from every statistic</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-v6-dark mt-7 mb-3">Honest limitations</h3>
    <ul class="space-y-2.5 list-none p-0 m-0">
      {limitations.map(([head, body]) => (
        <li class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed border-l-2 border-v6-border pl-4">
          <strong class="text-v6-dark">{head}</strong> {body}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <!-- FINDINGS, each paired with its chart rather than sending readers to an appendix. -->
    <h2 id="concentration" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Where does HubSpot renewal pain actually concentrate?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      Renewal-process pain is the most common thing teams describe (<strong>129 of 392</strong> trusted
      records, 33%), followed by integration (92, 23%), a catch-all "other" bucket (81, 21%), commissions
      (48, 12%), data quality (38, 10%), and agency overflow (4, 1%). But frequency and severity are not
      the same signal. Integration is less common than renewal yet far more acute: 70% of integration
      records are high-intensity versus 33% of renewal records. Read together, the corpus says renewals
      are where teams get stuck most often, and integrations are where they hurt most.
    </p>

    <ReportChart
      id="chart-1"
      title="Chart 1. Which pains show up most often"
      altText="Bar chart of pain categories by record count among 392 trusted customer-voice records: renewal 129, integration 92, other 81, commissions 48, data quality 38, agency overflow 4."
      bars={[
        { label: "Renewal", value: 129, display: "129", color: C.renewal },
        { label: "Integration", value: 92, display: "92", color: C.integration },
        { label: "Other", value: 81, display: "81", color: C.other },
        { label: "Commissions", value: 48, display: "48", color: C.commissions },
        { label: "Data quality", value: 38, display: "38", color: C.dataQuality },
        { label: "Agency overflow", value: 4, display: "4", lowBase: true },
      ]}
      table={{
        caption: "Source data for the chart above. Pain categories by frequency. Base: n=392.",
        headers: ["Pain category", "Records", "Share"],
        rows: [["Renewal","129","32.9%"],["Integration","92","23.5%"],["Other","81","20.7%"],["Commissions","48","12.2%"],["Data quality","38","9.7%"],["Agency overflow","4","1.0%"]],
      }}
    />

    <ReportChart
      id="chart-2"
      title="Chart 2. Which pains hurt most (acuteness)"
      unit="%"
      altText="Bar chart of acuteness, the share of records rated high-intensity, by pain category: integration 70%, data quality 42%, renewal 33%, other 25%, commissions 2%; agency overflow 100% is flagged as low base with only 4 records."
      bars={[
        { label: "Integration", value: 69.6, display: "69.6", color: C.integration },
        { label: "Data quality", value: 42.1, display: "42.1", color: C.dataQuality },
        { label: "Renewal", value: 32.6, display: "32.6", color: C.renewal },
        { label: "Other", value: 24.7, display: "24.7", color: C.other },
        { label: "Commissions", value: 2.1, display: "2.1", color: C.commissions },
        { label: "Agency overflow", value: 100, display: "100", lowBase: true },
      ]}
      table={{
        caption: "Source data for the chart above. Acuteness = share of a category rated high-intensity.",
        headers: ["Pain category", "High-intensity", "Category total", "Acuteness"],
        rows: [["Integration","64","92","69.6%"],["Data quality","16","38","42.1%"],["Renewal","42","129","32.6%"],["Other","20","81","24.7%"],["Commissions","1","48","2.1%"],["Agency overflow <em>(low base)</em>","4","4","100%"]],
      }}
      note="Agency overflow scores highest arithmetically but sits on only 4 records, so we disclose it and leave it out of the ranking."
    />

    <ReportChart
      id="chart-3"
      title="Chart 3. Where acute pain concentrates"
      altText="Chart splitting the 147 high-intensity records by pain category: integration 64, renewal 42, other 20, data quality 16, agency overflow 4, commissions 1."
      bars={[
        { label: "Integration", value: 64, display: "64", color: C.integration },
        { label: "Renewal", value: 42, display: "42", color: C.renewal },
        { label: "Other", value: 20, display: "20", color: C.other },
        { label: "Data quality", value: 16, display: "16", color: C.dataQuality },
        { label: "Agency overflow", value: 4, display: "4", lowBase: true },
        { label: "Commissions", value: 1, display: "1", color: C.commissions },
      ]}
      table={{
        caption: "Source data for the chart above. Composition of the 147 high-intensity trusted records.",
        headers: ["Pain category", "High-intensity records", "Share of 147"],
        rows: [["Integration","64","43.5%"],["Renewal","42","28.6%"],["Other","20","13.6%"],["Data quality","16","10.9%"],["Agency overflow","4","2.7%"],["Commissions","1","0.7%"]],
      }}
    />

    <h2 id="automation-gap" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Why can't teams just automate a HubSpot renewal?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      The most repeated story in the corpus is a team trying to auto-create next year's renewal deal and
      hitting a wall. HubSpot blocks deals created by a workflow from re-triggering that same workflow,
      so year-two renewals often generate but year-three renewals silently do not. Teams then bounce
      between paired workflows or fall back to spreadsheets. Renewal is the single most frequent pain
      (<strong>129 of 392</strong>, 33%), and 42 of those (33%) are already high-intensity.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "The enrolled deal was created by this workflow, so this action was skipped to prevent an infinite loop."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a RevOps leader on the HubSpot Community</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "How we could automate creation of renewal deal with close date 12 months after we have put current deal into Closed won state? Same company, same deal name, same sales person."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a poster on r/hubspot</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      A nuance worth keeping honest: not every renewal record is about building automation. A minority,
      mostly on r/hubspot, are complaints about HubSpot's own auto-renewal and pricing. We count these in
      the renewal category but flag them as a distinct flavour from the automation problem above.
    </p>

    <h2 id="line-items" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">What breaks a renewal after the deal is cloned?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      When teams do get a renewal deal to copy, <strong>the line items do not come with it</strong>.
      Cloning copies the amount and deal properties but leaves the products behind, so someone re-enters
      them by hand for every renewal. This is the mechanical root cause underneath the renewal-automation
      gap, and it dominates the HubSpot Community evidence.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "While it copies the 'Amount' and other info from the original deal, it does not copy the line items. This has to be done manually, which is extremely time-consuming and critical for issuing renewal quotes."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a RevOps leader on the HubSpot Community</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "I am setting up new deals for the year and having to click on it 12 times and then add the Start Date. Pretty time consuming and mind numbing."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">an operations admin on the HubSpot Community</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      The market's answer, deal-cloning apps, is well liked for exactly the narrow job it does, but
      reviewers also flag its limits: "out of order line items after copy, no customizability for
      excluding certain properties from the clone." Cloning solves the easy part; the renewal pipeline,
      at-risk visibility, and revenue math are the hard part.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accounting-sync" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Why is accounting-sync the most acute pain?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      Integration is where the corpus gets loudest. <strong>64 of 92</strong> integration records (70%)
      are high-intensity, and integration alone accounts for 44% of all acute pain in the study. The
      concentration is specific: 66 of 92 integration records (72%) name NetSuite, Xero, or QuickBooks,
      and the dedicated accounting-sync set (43 records) is 88% high-intensity. The pattern practitioners
      describe is a sync that works on record creation and then breaks the moment anyone edits either side.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "This is one of the hardest parts of a NetSuite to HubSpot integration, and it usually works just enough on create, then breaks down as soon as edits happen on either side."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a RevOps leader on the HubSpot Community</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "I attempted 125 deals and 54 were successful in creating a Sales Order while 71 failed."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a RevOps leader on the HubSpot Community (NetSuite)</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      The business consequence is not just annoyance; it is revenue data that finance cannot trust. Deals
      sync before their companies, invoices land against the wrong record, tax codes fail validation, and
      cumulative revenue does not roll up to the company level. That is the seam where renewal revenue and
      forecasting quietly leak.
    </p>

    <ReportChart
