    <h2 id="visibility" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Can teams even see their NRR and forecast?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      A distinct thread inside both renewal and data-quality records is that HubSpot's revenue math
      misfires on multi-year and ramped deals. When teams model escalating contracts as separate line
      items, <strong>HubSpot sums them and ignores the dates</strong>, inflating ARR, MRR, ACV, and the
      pipeline that leadership forecasts against.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "I am overstating my pipeline enormously on greenfield/growth deals, directly because of this mathematically incorrect handling."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">a RevOps leader on the HubSpot Community</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "Renewals get missed. Forecasting becomes unreliable. Customer Success lacks visibility. Escalation fees are tracked manually. Expansion opportunities are overlooked."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">internal interview note (revenue leader)</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      This is why the renewal problem is really a visibility problem. If the renewal deal never generates,
      or generates without line items, or carries revenue the system miscalculates, then net revenue
      retention and renewal forecasting are being run on numbers the team already knows are wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 id="commissions" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Is commissions part of the renewal problem too?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      Commissions shows a very different profile. It is reasonably common (<strong>48 of 392</strong>
      records, 12%) but rarely acute: only 1 of those 48 is high-intensity. Much of the discussion is
      design questions and spreadsheet fatigue rather than a system actively breaking. We include it for
      completeness, but the data does not support treating it as an equal-severity pain to renewal or
      accounting-sync.
    </p>

    <h2 id="hiring" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">What are teams actually hiring to fix?</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      Job descriptions are process evidence, not prevalence, so we put no percentage on them. But 54
      renewal-related postings show organisations funding roles whose entire purpose is to stop the leaks
      this study describes.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="mt-5 border-l-4 border-v6-copper pl-5 text-[15px] text-v6-text italic leading-relaxed">
      "Proactively identifying and executing on revenue leakage across the entire funnel, from lead generation through deal closure and renewal."
      <footer class="not-italic text-sm text-v6-text-muted mt-1.5">Revenue Operations Manager job description</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-v6-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
      When a company creates a full-time job to chase renewal leakage and reconcile CRM against the ledger
      by hand, the pain has crossed from annoyance into headcount.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rli" class="text-2xl font-bold text-v6-dark mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">The Renewal Leakage Index</h2>
    <p class="text-[15px] text-v6-text leading-relaxed">
      To rank where renewal revenue is most exposed, we combine the two signals this study measures: how
      often a pain shows up, and how acute it is when it does.
      <strong>This is a SwotBee construct, not an industry standard.</strong> For each pain category on
      the trusted 392 base, Frequency % is category records divided by 392, Acuteness % is high-intensity
      records divided by that category's total, and the Index is simply the two added together. We report
      both components so the score is never a black box, and exclude any category with fewer than about
      30 records as too thin to rank.
    </p>

